  "strategy": "Approach and rationale:\nThe central idea is to use an evolutionary approach combined with gradient-based optimization, targeting sparse comb sequences with specific periodic properties. We start with sparse initializations (minimal non-zero values) and then gradually add more information while keeping the sum constraint and non-negativity.\n\nMathematical specification:\nWe minimize the score: 2*n*\u2211|a_i a_j| / (sum(a))^2\nsubject to:\n- a_i \u2208 [0, 1000]\n- sum(a) \u2265 0.01\n\nThe convolution is defined by the full linear operation.\n\nImplementation procedure:\n```python\nfrom scipy.fft import rfft, irfft\nimport numpy.random as random\n\ndef propose_candidate(time_limit=1780):\n    fallback = [0] * 1024\n    fallback = [1/i for i in range(1, 1025)]\n    fallback = fallback / sum(fallback) * 0.1  # Ensure sum>=0.01, but with scale safety\n\n    n_val = 1024  # Base length\n    seq = fallback.copy()\n    best_seq = seq.copy()\n    best_score = evaluate_sequence(best_seq)  # evaluates the fallback\n    \n    # Timer to manage the algorithm\n    start_time = time.time()\n    remaining_time = time_limit - (start_time - time.time())\n\n    # --------------------- Initialization Phase ---------------------\n    # Use sparse comb initialization\n    if remaining_time > 10:\n        # Create uniform comb initialization\n        comb_intensity = 4.0  # Initial intensity\n        for n_size in [2**10, 2**11, 2**12]:\n            if time.time() > start_time + 30:\n                remaining_time = 0\n                break\n            \n            # Create sparse sequence with peaks at evenly distributed points\n            x = random.randint(0, n_size-1, n_size, dtype=float)\n            x = x / sum(x)  # Probability distribution for activation\n            \n            # Filter to keep only significant activations (minimal sum)\n            threshold = 0.001\n            if sum(x) < threshold:\n                continue\n                \n            seq = [0] * n_size\n            activating = random.rand(len(seq)) < x[i]\n            seq_init = [i if activating[i] else 0 for i in range(len(seq))]\n            seq_init = [max(0.0001, x) for x in seq_init]  # Ensure non-zero coefficients\n            seq_init = seq_init / sum(seq_init)  # Normalize\n\n            new_score = evaluate_sequence(seq_init.tolist())\n            if new_score < best_score:\n                best_score = new_score\n                best_seq = seq_init\n                print(f\"New best score found: {best_score} at length {n_size}\")\n\n        # Complete the uniform comb initialization\n        if sum(best_seq) > 0.01:  # Maintain the constraint\n            seq_used = best_seq[:]  # Not necessarily 1024 length now\n\n    # ------------------------------ Evolution Phase ------------------------------\n    if time.time() - start_time < time_limit * 0.9:\n        # Exploit gradient information in multiple scales\n        for t_updates in range(500):\n            if time.time() - start_time > time_limit * 0.99: break\n            \n            d_seq = np.diff(best_seq)\n            d_conv = np.convolve(d_seq, d_seq)\n            \n            # Consider using gradient information to update\n            # This is a placeholder; actual implementation will use detailed gradient-based evolution\n            mutations = random.normal(size=len(best_seq))\n            seq_candidate = best_seq + mutations*0.01\n            seq_candidate = np.clip(seq_candidate, 0, 1000)\n            seq_candidate = seq_candidate / sum(seq_candidate)  # Rescale\n            \n            new_score = evaluate_sequence(seq_candidate.tolist())\n            if new_score < best_score:\n                best_score = new_score\n                best_seq = seq_candidate\n                print(f\"Improvement found: best={best_score} after {t_updates+1} evolution steps\")\n        \n    # ------------------------------ Refinement Phase ------------------------------\n    # Use peak shaving to further enhance the sequence\n    auto_conv = np.convolve(best_seq, best_seq, mode=\"full\")\n    max_val = np.max(auto_conv)\n    \n    # Update individual coefficients to specifically target the maximum convolution point\n    # Calculate gradient contribution for each component\n    # This is represented in the equation above: 2*b[k]*a[k-i]\n    \n    # For each peak in auto_conv, distribute negative impact (but due to nonnegativity, we simply move mass)\n    # Implementation:\n    if time.time() - start_time < time_limit * 0.95:\n        # Detect peaks\n        peaks = np.where((auto_conv == max_val) | (auto_conv == max_val*0.99))[0]\n        for idx in peaks:\n            if time.time() - start_time > time_limit * 0.99: break\n            \n            # The convolution at idx is very high\n            # How to reduce: inspect symmetric points that contribute to this peak\n            # Specifically, a[i] and a[idx-i] if idx-i >= 0 and idx-i < len(best_seq)\n            \n            # We need to move away from the configurations that make this high\n            # This typically means redistributing mass from points directly contributing to the peak\n            pass  # Detailed implementation pending\n\n    return best_seq.tolist()\n```\n\nDetailed Evolution Implementation:\nWe will implement the following improvements:\n\n1. Use comb-shaped distributions to improve the score\n2. Optimize the intensity of peaks\n3. Ensure long-range dependency hardening\n\nAfter finishing the implementation, we should return the best sequence within the time limit.\n\nComputebudget androbustness:\nEach evolution step should be time-strict to not exceed the 1800s limit. We assume convolution operations (via FFT) are efficient.\n\nValidation and return contract:\nAfter each step, the sequence is evaluated with `evaluate_sequence` to ensure the metric improves.\n\nThis strategy should achieve better than the previous score of 1.504642.",
